---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/Diagram.astro';
import DBox from '../../components/DBox.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, '');
---

<Base
  title="Judgment calls, written down — Andrew Yang"
  description="A pricing engine that prefers blanks over guesses, an alert channel that must not report its own death, and a metric that measured the wrong thing."
>
  <p><a href={`${base}/lab/`}>← Lab</a></p>
  <span class="tag">design judgment</span>
  <h1>Judgment calls, written down</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    My LINE bots serve real users — a restaurant takes orders through one. Small
    systems, but production is production: money is counted, alerts wake
    people, and AI output meets reality. Three design calls from that world,
    each one a rule I now carry.
  </p>

  <Diagram
    viewBox="0 0 920 172"
    alt="A customer's free-form message goes to an LLM, which proposes order lines. The proposal enters a deterministic pricing engine with an explicit source priority: customer-stated price, then daily menu, then lexicon. Two exits: a priced total when a source resolves, or — on the dashed path — a blank left for a human when nothing does. There is no path on which the model's guess becomes a price."
    caption="The flagship rule in one picture: the AI proposes, the engine is the guardrail, and the dashed exit — a blank — is a feature, not a failure."
  >
    <DBox x={10} y={56} w={190} h={60} title="customer message" sub="free-form chat" />
    <DBox x={240} y={56} w={190} h={60} title="LLM proposes" sub="order lines — helpful," sub2="sometimes wrong" />
    <DBox x={470} y={56} w={200} h={60} accent title="deterministic engine" sub="stated price → daily menu" sub2="→ lexicon, in that order" />
    <DBox x={710} y={10} w={200} h={60} title="a priced total" sub="a source resolved it" />
    <DBox x={710} y={102} w={200} h={60} dashed title="a blank, for a human" sub="no source did —" sub2="never the model's guess" />
    <path class="flow" d="M200,86 L234,86" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <path class="flow" d="M430,86 L464,86" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <path class="flow" d="M670,76 L690,76 L690,40 L704,40" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
    <path class="flow dash" d="M670,96 L690,96 L690,132 L704,132" marker-end="url(#d-arrow)" />
  </Diagram>

  <h2>1. Prefer a blank over a guess</h2>
  <p>
    An ordering bot uses an LLM to parse free-form customer messages into order
    lines. Reconciliation against the restaurant's books surfaced the failure
    class: the model would "helpfully" normalize a vague item name onto the
    menu — turning a customer's "assorted, 800" into a menu item priced 210.
  </p>
  <p>
    The fix was not a better prompt. The pricing engine became deterministic
    with an explicit priority order — customer-stated price, then daily menu,
    then lexicon — and a hard rule: <strong>if no source resolves it, the total
    stays blank for a human</strong>. Platter-style words never fall back to
    menu prices at all. The AI proposes; the engine is the guardrail; blanks
    are cheaper than wrong totals.
  </p>

  <h2>2. The broken thing must not report its own death</h2>
  <p>
    Engineering alerts once shared a bot with the product features they watched
    over — meaning a dead bot would be responsible for announcing that it was
    dead. Alerts moved to a dedicated ops bot, separated from the
    conversational one, so a product-side failure cannot silence its own alarm.
    The same principle, one level up, gave the monitoring stack an
    <a href={`${base}/lab/one-machine-one-operator/`}>external dead-man
    switch</a> — the chain of "who watches X" has to terminate somewhere off
    the machine.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Ask what a metric actually measures</h2>
  <p>
    The obvious silence alarm — "no webhook events for N hours" — measures user
    activity, not service health: a chat platform only delivers webhooks when
    someone talks. For a low-traffic bot it alarms on quiet days and misses
    real deaths after restarts, because the metric resets with the process. The
    replacement watches config-presence and rejection counters — signals that
    exist independently of whether users happen to be talking — and the
    long-term answer (a database-backed freshness gauge) is recorded as a known
    limitation next to the code, so nobody rediscovers it the hard way.
  </p>

  <h2>The pattern</h2>
  <p>
    None of these are technology decisions — the stack is ordinary. Each is a
    judgment about <em>failure asymmetry</em>: a blank beats a wrong price, a
    redundant channel beats an eloquent corpse, a boring-but-honest metric
    beats a rich-but-lying one. Writing the judgment down where the code lives —
    with the incident that motivated it — is what makes it transferable.
  </p>

  <h2>What I'd tell my past self</h2>
  <p>
    When AI is in the loop, design the system so that the AI's confidence is
    an input, never a verdict. Every place I let a model's output flow straight
    into a number someone trusts, reconciliation eventually found the cost of
    that shortcut.
  </p>
</Base>
